# treedemo: the Tree widget driving Tea v2 under a real pty (S10).
#
# The view is a live "cur=<y> <value>:<open>" header above the tree, so every
# state has a UNIQUE needle.  The demo app keeps a scalar model and rebuilds
# the widget transiently (the full Tree.Model exceeds the pty buffer), so the
# needles also prove the rebuild restores selection + open flags exactly.
# Coverage: j walks the preorder over VISIBLE nodes (a closed parent's
# children are skipped: h on bash makes the next j land on carlos), h/l
# close/open the selected node, enter toggles the root, g/G jump to the
# top/bottom of the visible tree, ? flips short->full help (the full block's
# "½ page down" desc is the needle) and back.  The flip-BACK needle is the
# differential renderer's SHRINK-CLEAR \e[15;1H\e[J (the 18-row full-help
# frame collapsing to the 14-row short frame): the short help line itself is
# SGR-wrapped (\e[38;...m⏎\e[0m toggle — no plain "⏎ toggle" substring) and
# its first-paint twin would stale-match, while \e[J is emitted exactly once,
# when a frame shrinks.  q quits via the TaskQuit scan.
#
# The tree rows render through the DEFAULT dark styleset (SGR bytes between
# every cell — see treeunit for the byte-exact stripped render), so the
# needles here are the PLAIN header/substrings only; trailing-space row
# padding is never asserted (R4).
#
# Escapes: \e[?25l hide cursor, \e[?25h show cursor.
expect \e[?25l\e[2Kcur=0 ~/charm:1\r\n
send j
expect cur=1 ayman:0
send j
expect cur=2 bash:1
send h
expect cur=2 bash:0
send j
expect cur=3 carlos:1
send G
expect cur=7 maas:0
send g
expect cur=0 ~/charm:1
send \r
expect cur=0 ~/charm:0
send l
expect cur=0 ~/charm:1
send ?
expect ½ page down
send ?
expect \e[15;1H\e[J
send q
expect \e[?25h
expect_exit 0
